<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kanesh</h1><p class="st-meta">2000 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.) · capital Kanesh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kanesh, a central Anatolian city-kingdom near Kayseri that hosted the largest Assyrian trading colony, and whose name gave the Hittite language its own title.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kanesh, at Kültepe, was a native Anatolian city by the late 3rd millennium BCE. From c. 1950 BCE merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> settled in a trading quarter, the karum, below its citadel, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and woollen textiles by donkey caravan to exchange for Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Local kings and queens ruled the citadel and taxed the trade under treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, while the merchants kept their own court and over 23,000 letters and contracts in Old Assyrian. The archives record Anatolian personal names that are the earliest evidence of Hittite, which its speakers later called nesili after Nesa, the local name of Kanesh.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1750 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithana">Pithana</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta of Kussara</a> captured Nesa and made it their seat. The trade colony faded c. 1700 BCE and the city lost importance as power shifted towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kanesh.jpg" alt="Kanesh" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%BCltepeOberstadtPalast.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2000 BCE – 1700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kanesh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Warshama, Pithana, Anitta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anatolian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hittite, Old Assyrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Kanesh, Nesa, Kultepe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1850&amp;focus=kanesh" aria-label="Kanesh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5564 784 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kanesh lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5884 1040L5927 1023M5884 1040L5852 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1023h0M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5884 1040h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5884 1040h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1850&amp;focus=kanesh">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
